1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,753
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:01,933 --> 00:00:04,446
There's a number in personal finance that

3
00:00:04,446 --> 00:00:06,101
quietly changes everything.

4
00:00:06,281 --> 00:00:08,069
It's not a million dollars.

5
00:00:08,249 --> 00:00:10,234
It's not even one hundred thousand.

6
00:00:10,414 --> 00:00:12,051
It's ten thousand dollars.

7
00:00:12,231 --> 00:00:14,746
When people finally get their first ten

8
00:00:14,746 --> 00:00:17,067
thousand dollars saved and invested,

9
00:00:17,067 --> 00:00:18,164
something shifts.

10
00:00:18,344 --> 00:00:19,532
The math changes,

11
00:00:19,532 --> 00:00:21,141
the psychology changes,

12
00:00:21,141 --> 00:00:23,868
and the next ten thousand starts coming

13
00:00:23,868 --> 00:00:24,358
faster.

14
00:00:24,538 --> 00:00:26,359
Today I'm going to show you why,

15
00:00:26,359 --> 00:00:28,009
and exactly how to get there.

16
00:00:28,359 --> 00:00:30,425
Here's the rule in one sentence.

17
00:00:30,605 --> 00:00:32,956
Your first ten thousand dollars is the

18
00:00:32,956 --> 00:00:35,432
hardest, and every ten thousand after it

19
00:00:35,432 --> 00:00:36,236
comes faster.

20
00:00:36,416 --> 00:00:38,995
Investing five hundred dollars a month at

21
00:00:38,995 --> 00:00:41,386
a seven percent average yearly return,

22
00:00:41,386 --> 00:00:44,091
the first ten thousand takes about nineteen

23
00:00:44,091 --> 00:00:44,531
months.

24
00:00:44,711 --> 00:00:47,161
The tenth ten thousand takes about nine.

25
00:00:47,511 --> 00:00:48,544
Why does that happen?

26
00:00:48,724 --> 00:00:49,836
Because at first,

27
00:00:49,836 --> 00:00:52,126
you're doing all the work yourself.

28
00:00:52,306 --> 00:00:54,129
Every dollar in the account is a

29
00:00:54,129 --> 00:00:55,155
dollar you earned,

30
00:00:55,155 --> 00:00:56,067
saved and moved.

31
00:00:56,247 --> 00:00:58,440
But once the balance gets bigger,

32
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:00,566
the money itself starts helping.

33
00:01:00,746 --> 00:01:02,617
Growth that was a few dollars a

34
00:01:02,617 --> 00:01:03,884
month becomes dozens,

35
00:01:03,884 --> 00:01:04,728
then hundreds.

36
00:01:04,908 --> 00:01:07,307
Ten thousand dollars is roughly where most

37
00:01:07,307 --> 00:01:09,535
people first start to notice that help,

38
00:01:09,535 --> 00:01:11,249
and that's why it feels like a

39
00:01:11,249 --> 00:01:12,048
turning point.

40
00:01:12,399 --> 00:01:14,720
We'll look at this from three angles.

41
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:16,352
First, the numbers.

42
00:01:16,532 --> 00:01:18,980
What ten thousand dollars really does,

43
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:21,171
how fast the next milestones come,

44
00:01:21,171 --> 00:01:23,233
and what happens when it sits in

45
00:01:23,233 --> 00:01:24,264
the wrong place.

46
00:01:24,444 --> 00:01:26,441
Second, the psychology.

47
00:01:26,621 --> 00:01:29,209
Why the first ten thousand feels impossible,

48
00:01:29,209 --> 00:01:31,209
and what changes in your head once

49
00:01:31,209 --> 00:01:31,915
you have it.

50
00:01:32,095 --> 00:01:33,674
And third, the playbook.

51
00:01:33,854 --> 00:01:35,654
A step by step plan to reach

52
00:01:35,654 --> 00:01:37,198
your first ten thousand,

53
00:01:37,198 --> 00:01:38,870
protect it, and repeat it.

54
00:01:39,050 --> 00:01:42,242
Quick reminder, all growth examples here are

55
00:01:42,242 --> 00:01:44,274
illustrations, not promises.

56
00:01:44,623 --> 00:01:46,226
Let's start with the numbers.

57
00:01:46,406 --> 00:01:48,164
And the first lesson is what ten

58
00:01:48,164 --> 00:01:50,307
thousand dollars actually does for you.

59
00:01:50,657 --> 00:01:51,597
Our numbers.

60
00:01:51,777 --> 00:01:55,010
All growth examples use monthly compounding with

61
00:01:55,010 --> 00:01:57,098
a stated average yearly return,

62
00:01:57,098 --> 00:01:58,580
usually seven percent.

63
00:01:58,760 --> 00:02:00,381
These are illustrations,

64
00:02:00,381 --> 00:02:03,284
not predictions, because real markets go up

65
00:02:03,284 --> 00:02:03,892
and down.

66
00:02:04,072 --> 00:02:07,126
Emergency savings data comes from the Federal

67
00:02:07,126 --> 00:02:11,131
Reserve's Survey of Household Economics and Decisionmaking.

68
00:02:11,311 --> 00:02:13,527
Credit card rates come from the Federal

69
00:02:13,527 --> 00:02:13,981
Reserve.

70
00:02:14,161 --> 00:02:16,321
Account rules come from the IRS.

71
00:02:16,501 --> 00:02:17,867
This is education,

72
00:02:17,867 --> 00:02:19,461
not financial advice.

73
00:02:19,812 --> 00:02:20,589
Angle one.

74
00:02:20,769 --> 00:02:21,570
The numbers.

75
00:02:21,750 --> 00:02:24,528
Seven lessons about what ten thousand dollars

76
00:02:24,528 --> 00:02:25,268
really does.

77
00:02:25,619 --> 00:02:26,756
Lesson number one.

78
00:02:26,936 --> 00:02:28,426
At ten thousand dollars,

79
00:02:28,426 --> 00:02:29,792
your money gets a job.

80
00:02:30,142 --> 00:02:32,585
At a seven percent average yearly return,

81
00:02:32,585 --> 00:02:35,444
ten thousand dollars earns roughly seven hundred

82
00:02:35,444 --> 00:02:36,516
dollars in a year.

83
00:02:36,696 --> 00:02:38,511
That's about fifty eight dollars a month

84
00:02:38,511 --> 00:02:39,645
that you didn't work for.

85
00:02:39,825 --> 00:02:41,787
It won't change your life by itself.

86
00:02:41,967 --> 00:02:42,903
But it's a real,

87
00:02:42,903 --> 00:02:45,535
small paycheck coming from your money instead

88
00:02:45,535 --> 00:02:46,588
of from your time.

89
00:02:46,768 --> 00:02:48,019
And for most people,

90
00:02:48,019 --> 00:02:50,083
it's the first time that has ever

91
00:02:50,083 --> 00:02:50,646
happened.

92
00:02:50,996 --> 00:02:53,049
Compare that to one thousand dollars,

93
00:02:53,049 --> 00:02:55,381
which earns roughly seventy dollars a year

94
00:02:55,381 --> 00:02:56,324
at the same rate.

95
00:02:56,504 --> 00:02:58,954
Seventy dollars is easy to ignore.

96
00:02:59,134 --> 00:03:00,678
Seven hundred is not.

97
00:03:00,858 --> 00:03:02,321
It's a car payment,

98
00:03:02,321 --> 00:03:03,476
a utility bill,

99
00:03:03,476 --> 00:03:04,632
a weekend trip.

100
00:03:04,812 --> 00:03:07,603
Ten thousand is roughly where investment growth

101
00:03:07,603 --> 00:03:09,622
goes from invisible to noticeable,

102
00:03:09,622 --> 00:03:11,760
and noticing it changes how you feel

103
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:12,532
about saving.

104
00:03:12,882 --> 00:03:15,643
And remember, that seven hundred dollars doesn't

105
00:03:15,643 --> 00:03:16,621
have to be spent.

106
00:03:16,801 --> 00:03:18,252
If you leave it invested,

107
00:03:18,252 --> 00:03:20,226
it earns its own growth next year.

108
00:03:20,406 --> 00:03:22,577
Your money's job is to hire more

109
00:03:22,577 --> 00:03:25,630
workers, and those workers hire more workers.

110
00:03:25,810 --> 00:03:28,616
That's compounding, and ten thousand dollars is

111
00:03:28,616 --> 00:03:30,884
when the hiring really starts to show.

112
00:03:31,234 --> 00:03:32,000
Try this.

113
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:34,656
Calculate seven percent of whatever you have

114
00:03:34,656 --> 00:03:35,500
invested today.

115
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:37,613
That's roughly what your money might earn

116
00:03:37,613 --> 00:03:38,838
for you in a typical year.

117
00:03:39,018 --> 00:03:39,935
Write it down.

118
00:03:40,115 --> 00:03:42,213
Watching that number grow can become one

119
00:03:42,213 --> 00:03:44,205
of the most motivating numbers in your

120
00:03:44,205 --> 00:03:44,992
financial life.

121
00:03:45,341 --> 00:03:46,607
Lesson number two.

122
00:03:46,787 --> 00:03:49,277
Each ten thousand comes faster than the

123
00:03:49,277 --> 00:03:49,597
last.

124
00:03:49,946 --> 00:03:52,868
Let's follow one person investing five hundred

125
00:03:52,868 --> 00:03:55,028
dollars a month at a seven percent

126
00:03:55,028 --> 00:03:56,425
average yearly return.

127
00:03:56,605 --> 00:03:59,151
The first ten thousand takes about nineteen

128
00:03:59,151 --> 00:03:59,565
months.

129
00:03:59,745 --> 00:04:02,439
The second ten thousand takes about eighteen.

130
00:04:02,619 --> 00:04:04,488
The third, about fifteen.

131
00:04:04,668 --> 00:04:06,318
By the fifth ten thousand,

132
00:04:06,318 --> 00:04:08,030
it's about fourteen months,

133
00:04:08,030 --> 00:04:09,997
and the timing keeps shrinking.

134
00:04:10,347 --> 00:04:12,066
Here's that journey on one chart.

135
00:04:12,246 --> 00:04:14,123
Five hundred dollars a month,

136
00:04:14,123 --> 00:04:15,223
for eleven years,

137
00:04:15,223 --> 00:04:17,877
at a seven percent average yearly return.

138
00:04:18,057 --> 00:04:20,201
Watch how the green line pulls away

139
00:04:20,201 --> 00:04:21,487
from the dotted line,

140
00:04:21,487 --> 00:04:23,264
which is the money you put in

141
00:04:23,264 --> 00:04:23,815
yourself.

142
00:04:24,165 --> 00:04:26,102
By the time this person goes from

143
00:04:26,102 --> 00:04:28,919
ninety thousand to one hundred thousand dollars,

144
00:04:28,919 --> 00:04:31,208
that ten thousand takes only about nine

145
00:04:31,208 --> 00:04:31,619
months.

146
00:04:31,799 --> 00:04:33,773
Same five hundred dollars a month.

147
00:04:33,953 --> 00:04:34,777
Same effort.

148
00:04:34,957 --> 00:04:36,492
But the money already in the account

149
00:04:36,492 --> 00:04:37,728
is doing more and more of the

150
00:04:37,728 --> 00:04:38,069
lifting.

151
00:04:38,249 --> 00:04:40,608
The whole journey to one hundred thousand

152
00:04:40,608 --> 00:04:42,161
takes roughly eleven years.

153
00:04:42,511 --> 00:04:44,095
This is why Charlie Munger,

154
00:04:44,095 --> 00:04:46,090
Warren Buffett's longtime partner,

155
00:04:46,090 --> 00:04:48,789
reportedly said the first one hundred thousand

156
00:04:48,789 --> 00:04:50,079
is a pain in the neck,

157
00:04:50,079 --> 00:04:51,370
but you have to do it.

158
00:04:51,550 --> 00:04:54,388
The early stretch feels slow because compounding

159
00:04:54,388 --> 00:04:56,043
hasn't had time to help yet.

160
00:04:56,223 --> 00:04:58,241
The first ten thousand is the first

161
00:04:58,241 --> 00:04:59,683
sign that help is coming.

162
00:05:00,032 --> 00:05:00,845
Try this.

163
00:05:01,025 --> 00:05:03,095
Make a simple milestone chart,

164
00:05:03,095 --> 00:05:05,164
ten thousand, twenty thousand,

165
00:05:05,164 --> 00:05:06,958
thirty thousand and so on.

166
00:05:07,138 --> 00:05:08,594
Put the date you hit each one

167
00:05:08,594 --> 00:05:09,146
next to it.

168
00:05:09,326 --> 00:05:11,844
Over time, you'll see the gaps between

169
00:05:11,844 --> 00:05:14,361
dates shrink, and that visual proof is

170
00:05:14,361 --> 00:05:14,957
powerful.

171
00:05:15,307 --> 00:05:16,538
Lesson number three.

172
00:05:16,718 --> 00:05:18,088
The crossover point.

173
00:05:18,439 --> 00:05:21,034
There's a moment in every investing journey

174
00:05:21,034 --> 00:05:22,363
that feels like magic.

175
00:05:22,543 --> 00:05:24,622
It's the year when your investment growth

176
00:05:24,622 --> 00:05:26,245
is bigger than the money you put

177
00:05:26,245 --> 00:05:26,397
in.

178
00:05:26,577 --> 00:05:28,857
For our five hundred dollars a month

179
00:05:28,857 --> 00:05:31,642
investor, that's six thousand dollars a year

180
00:05:31,642 --> 00:05:32,719
in contributions.

181
00:05:32,899 --> 00:05:34,016
At seven percent,

182
00:05:34,016 --> 00:05:36,776
growth reaches six thousand dollars a year

183
00:05:36,776 --> 00:05:38,748
when the balance is in the low

184
00:05:38,748 --> 00:05:39,865
eighty thousands.

185
00:05:40,215 --> 00:05:41,341
In this example,

186
00:05:41,341 --> 00:05:43,384
that takes roughly ten years.

187
00:05:43,564 --> 00:05:44,583
After the crossover,

188
00:05:44,583 --> 00:05:46,416
your money is earning more each year

189
00:05:46,416 --> 00:05:47,384
than you're saving.

190
00:05:47,564 --> 00:05:48,528
If you kept going,

191
00:05:48,528 --> 00:05:50,188
the gap would widen every year.

192
00:05:50,368 --> 00:05:53,415
Eventually, contributions become a small part of

193
00:05:53,415 --> 00:05:55,828
the story, and growth becomes the main

194
00:05:55,828 --> 00:05:56,463
character.

195
00:05:56,813 --> 00:05:59,727
Of course, real markets don't deliver seven

196
00:05:59,727 --> 00:06:01,015
percent every year.

197
00:06:01,195 --> 00:06:02,684
Some years are up a lot,

198
00:06:02,684 --> 00:06:03,552
some are down.

199
00:06:03,732 --> 00:06:05,220
But over long periods,

200
00:06:05,220 --> 00:06:06,844
the pattern is the same.

201
00:06:07,024 --> 00:06:08,855
The balance has to grow before the

202
00:06:08,855 --> 00:06:09,717
growth can grow.

203
00:06:09,897 --> 00:06:12,102
And ten thousand dollars is the first

204
00:06:12,102 --> 00:06:13,829
real step on that road to the

205
00:06:13,829 --> 00:06:14,425
crossover.

206
00:06:14,775 --> 00:06:15,622
Try this.

207
00:06:15,802 --> 00:06:18,948
Divide your yearly contributions by seven percent.

208
00:06:19,128 --> 00:06:21,836
That's roughly the balance where your crossover

209
00:06:21,836 --> 00:06:22,240
begins.

210
00:06:22,420 --> 00:06:24,718
If you invest six thousand a year,

211
00:06:24,718 --> 00:06:26,948
it's around eighty five thousand.

212
00:06:27,128 --> 00:06:28,897
Now you have a clear long term

213
00:06:28,897 --> 00:06:29,310
target.

214
00:06:29,660 --> 00:06:30,926
Lesson number four.

215
00:06:31,106 --> 00:06:34,090
Where your ten thousand sits matters enormously.

216
00:06:34,439 --> 00:06:37,270
Imagine you leave ten thousand dollars alone

217
00:06:37,270 --> 00:06:38,364
for thirty years.

218
00:06:38,544 --> 00:06:39,500
At two percent,

219
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:42,368
roughly what some savings accounts have paid,

220
00:06:42,368 --> 00:06:44,662
it grows to about eighteen thousand.

221
00:06:44,842 --> 00:06:45,817
At four percent,

222
00:06:45,817 --> 00:06:47,524
about thirty three thousand.

223
00:06:47,704 --> 00:06:48,920
At seven percent,

224
00:06:48,920 --> 00:06:50,781
about eighty one thousand.

225
00:06:50,961 --> 00:06:52,010
At ten percent,

226
00:06:52,010 --> 00:06:53,968
almost two hundred thousand.

227
00:06:54,318 --> 00:06:56,152
Same ten thousand dollars.

228
00:06:56,332 --> 00:06:57,690
Same thirty years.

229
00:06:57,870 --> 00:07:00,041
The only difference is the average return.

230
00:07:00,221 --> 00:07:01,854
That's a gap of more than one

231
00:07:01,854 --> 00:07:04,612
hundred sixty thousand dollars between the lowest

232
00:07:04,612 --> 00:07:05,794
and highest examples.

233
00:07:05,974 --> 00:07:08,664
Of course, higher returns usually come with

234
00:07:08,664 --> 00:07:09,853
more ups and downs,

235
00:07:09,853 --> 00:07:12,293
so this isn't about chasing the highest

236
00:07:12,293 --> 00:07:12,731
number.

237
00:07:12,911 --> 00:07:14,990
It's about matching the money to its

238
00:07:14,990 --> 00:07:15,221
job.

239
00:07:15,572 --> 00:07:17,468
Money you'll need in the next year

240
00:07:17,468 --> 00:07:19,922
or two belongs somewhere safe and available,

241
00:07:19,922 --> 00:07:21,818
like a high yield savings account.

242
00:07:21,998 --> 00:07:24,372
Money you won't touch for decades usually

243
00:07:24,372 --> 00:07:26,978
belongs in diversified long term investments.

244
00:07:27,158 --> 00:07:29,707
The mistake is leaving decades long money

245
00:07:29,707 --> 00:07:32,255
in places designed for short term safety.

246
00:07:32,606 --> 00:07:33,395
Try this.

247
00:07:33,575 --> 00:07:36,071
Split your savings into two labels.

248
00:07:36,251 --> 00:07:37,913
Need it within three years,

249
00:07:37,913 --> 00:07:39,760
and won't need it for ten plus

250
00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:40,129
years.

251
00:07:40,309 --> 00:07:42,346
Then check whether each pile is sitting

252
00:07:42,346 --> 00:07:43,862
in the right kind of account.

253
00:07:44,212 --> 00:07:45,558
Lesson number five.

254
00:07:45,738 --> 00:07:48,200
Idle cash slowly loses value.

255
00:07:48,550 --> 00:07:51,181
If inflation averages three percent a year,

256
00:07:51,181 --> 00:07:53,445
ten thousand dollars kept in cash for

257
00:07:53,445 --> 00:07:55,709
twenty years will buy only what about

258
00:07:55,709 --> 00:07:58,035
fifty five hundred dollars buys today.

259
00:07:58,215 --> 00:08:00,543
The number on the screen still says

260
00:08:00,543 --> 00:08:01,408
ten thousand.

261
00:08:01,588 --> 00:08:03,285
But what it can buy has nearly

262
00:08:03,285 --> 00:08:04,246
been cut in half.

263
00:08:04,597 --> 00:08:06,731
This is the hidden cost of playing

264
00:08:06,731 --> 00:08:08,300
it too safe for too long.

265
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:11,109
Cash feels safe because the number never

266
00:08:11,109 --> 00:08:11,766
goes down.

267
00:08:11,946 --> 00:08:13,931
But inflation is a slow leak.

268
00:08:14,111 --> 00:08:16,276
You don't notice it month to month,

269
00:08:16,276 --> 00:08:18,440
only when you look back years later

270
00:08:18,440 --> 00:08:20,543
and realize everything costs more.

271
00:08:20,893 --> 00:08:22,692
That doesn't mean cash is bad.

272
00:08:22,872 --> 00:08:25,195
You need cash for emergencies and near

273
00:08:25,195 --> 00:08:25,867
term goals.

274
00:08:26,047 --> 00:08:28,307
It just means long term money needs

275
00:08:28,307 --> 00:08:30,697
to grow faster than inflation to keep

276
00:08:30,697 --> 00:08:31,794
its buying power.

277
00:08:31,974 --> 00:08:35,518
Otherwise, you're getting poorer slowly while feeling

278
00:08:35,518 --> 00:08:35,852
safe.

279
00:08:36,203 --> 00:08:37,027
Try this.

280
00:08:37,207 --> 00:08:38,927
Look up the current yield on your

281
00:08:38,927 --> 00:08:39,761
savings account.

282
00:08:39,941 --> 00:08:42,372
If it's well below the recent inflation

283
00:08:42,372 --> 00:08:44,928
rate, consider whether some of that money

284
00:08:44,928 --> 00:08:46,361
belongs somewhere else.

285
00:08:46,712 --> 00:08:48,047
Lesson number six.

286
00:08:48,227 --> 00:08:50,804
Ten thousand dollars is a powerful shield.

287
00:08:51,154 --> 00:08:54,573
The Federal Reserve's Survey of Household Economics

288
00:08:54,573 --> 00:08:58,193
and Decisionmaking regularly asks Americans how they'd

289
00:08:58,193 --> 00:09:01,277
cover a four hundred dollar emergency expense.

290
00:09:01,457 --> 00:09:02,516
In recent years,

291
00:09:02,516 --> 00:09:04,766
only around six in ten adults said

292
00:09:04,766 --> 00:09:06,883
they'd cover it with cash or its

293
00:09:06,883 --> 00:09:07,611
equivalent.

294
00:09:07,791 --> 00:09:09,600
Many others said they'd borrow,

295
00:09:09,600 --> 00:09:11,818
sell something or couldn't cover it at

296
00:09:11,818 --> 00:09:12,052
all.

297
00:09:12,401 --> 00:09:15,356
Now imagine having ten thousand dollars set

298
00:09:15,356 --> 00:09:15,768
aside.

299
00:09:15,948 --> 00:09:17,057
A car repair,

300
00:09:17,057 --> 00:09:18,337
a medical bill,

301
00:09:18,337 --> 00:09:19,873
a sudden job loss.

302
00:09:20,053 --> 00:09:22,357
Instead of reaching for a credit card

303
00:09:22,357 --> 00:09:24,039
at twenty percent interest,

304
00:09:24,039 --> 00:09:25,347
you handle it calmly.

305
00:09:25,527 --> 00:09:29,695
Ten thousand dollars turns emergencies into inconveniences.

306
00:09:30,044 --> 00:09:32,053
It also protects your investments.

307
00:09:32,233 --> 00:09:34,770
People without cash reserves often have to

308
00:09:34,770 --> 00:09:36,884
sell investments at the worst time,

309
00:09:36,884 --> 00:09:38,515
like during a market crash,

310
00:09:38,515 --> 00:09:39,965
to pay unexpected bills.

311
00:09:40,145 --> 00:09:42,477
A cash cushion lets your long term

312
00:09:42,477 --> 00:09:45,288
money stay invested and keep compounding.

313
00:09:45,639 --> 00:09:46,591
Try this.

314
00:09:46,771 --> 00:09:50,228
Figure out your essential monthly expenses,

315
00:09:50,228 --> 00:09:52,238
housing, food, utilities,

316
00:09:52,238 --> 00:09:54,329
insurance, transportation.

317
00:09:54,509 --> 00:09:55,948
Multiply by three.

318
00:09:56,128 --> 00:09:58,868
That's a solid starting emergency fund target.

319
00:09:59,219 --> 00:10:00,565
Lesson number seven.

320
00:10:00,745 --> 00:10:02,614
The rule works in reverse too.

321
00:10:02,964 --> 00:10:05,833
Ten thousand dollars invested at seven percent

322
00:10:05,833 --> 00:10:08,202
can earn about seven hundred dollars a

323
00:10:08,202 --> 00:10:08,514
year.

324
00:10:08,694 --> 00:10:11,082
But ten thousand dollars of credit card

325
00:10:11,082 --> 00:10:13,226
debt at twenty two percent can cost

326
00:10:13,226 --> 00:10:15,675
about two thousand two hundred dollars a

327
00:10:15,675 --> 00:10:16,717
year in interest.

328
00:10:16,897 --> 00:10:19,311
Same number, very different direction.

329
00:10:19,661 --> 00:10:22,004
Credit card rates in the United States

330
00:10:22,004 --> 00:10:24,532
have been above twenty percent on average

331
00:10:24,532 --> 00:10:25,518
in recent years,

332
00:10:25,518 --> 00:10:27,614
according to Federal Reserve data.

333
00:10:27,794 --> 00:10:28,654
At that rate,

334
00:10:28,654 --> 00:10:31,099
a balance grows much faster than most

335
00:10:31,099 --> 00:10:31,893
investments.

336
00:10:32,073 --> 00:10:33,941
And if you only pay the minimum,

337
00:10:33,941 --> 00:10:35,925
the balance can take many years to

338
00:10:35,925 --> 00:10:36,275
clear.

339
00:10:36,625 --> 00:10:37,968
So for many people,

340
00:10:37,968 --> 00:10:40,795
the real ten thousand dollar rule starts

341
00:10:40,795 --> 00:10:41,502
with debt.

342
00:10:41,682 --> 00:10:44,081
Getting from minus ten thousand to zero

343
00:10:44,081 --> 00:10:46,234
is just as powerful as getting from

344
00:10:46,234 --> 00:10:47,526
zero to ten thousand,

345
00:10:47,526 --> 00:10:48,880
and often more urgent.

346
00:10:49,060 --> 00:10:51,297
Every dollar of high interest debt you

347
00:10:51,297 --> 00:10:53,415
pay off is a guaranteed return equal

348
00:10:53,415 --> 00:10:54,122
to its rate.

349
00:10:54,471 --> 00:10:55,284
Try this.

350
00:10:55,464 --> 00:10:57,916
Add up every high interest balance you

351
00:10:57,916 --> 00:10:58,239
have.

352
00:10:58,419 --> 00:10:59,329
If it's large,

353
00:10:59,329 --> 00:11:02,320
your first milestone isn't ten thousand saved.

354
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:03,614
It's zero owed.

355
00:11:03,964 --> 00:11:04,963
That's angle one.

356
00:11:05,143 --> 00:11:06,821
Your money gets a job,

357
00:11:06,821 --> 00:11:08,346
milestones speed up,

358
00:11:08,346 --> 00:11:09,871
the crossover point,

359
00:11:09,871 --> 00:11:11,167
where money sits,

360
00:11:11,167 --> 00:11:13,607
inflation, the emergency shield,

361
00:11:13,607 --> 00:11:15,209
and the reverse rule.

362
00:11:15,389 --> 00:11:16,631
Now angle two.

363
00:11:16,811 --> 00:11:17,879
The psychology.

364
00:11:18,229 --> 00:11:20,810
Getting to ten thousand dollars isn't mainly

365
00:11:20,810 --> 00:11:21,689
a math problem.

366
00:11:21,869 --> 00:11:23,251
It's a behavior problem.

367
00:11:23,431 --> 00:11:25,456
Plenty of people earn enough to save

368
00:11:25,456 --> 00:11:26,357
it but never do.

369
00:11:26,537 --> 00:11:28,467
And plenty of people who save it

370
00:11:28,467 --> 00:11:29,613
end up spending it.

371
00:11:29,793 --> 00:11:30,628
In this angle,

372
00:11:30,628 --> 00:11:32,894
we'll look at seven mental forces that

373
00:11:32,894 --> 00:11:35,219
decide whether you reach your first ten

374
00:11:35,219 --> 00:11:37,247
thousand, and whether you keep it.

375
00:11:37,596 --> 00:11:38,827
Lesson number eight.

376
00:11:39,007 --> 00:11:41,178
Early progress feels invisible.

377
00:11:41,528 --> 00:11:44,231
When you're saving your first ten thousand,

378
00:11:44,231 --> 00:11:45,174
growth is tiny.

379
00:11:45,354 --> 00:11:46,273
In the first year,

380
00:11:46,273 --> 00:11:48,264
you might earn a couple hundred dollars

381
00:11:48,264 --> 00:11:48,825
in returns.

382
00:11:49,005 --> 00:11:51,319
Almost everything in the account is money

383
00:11:51,319 --> 00:11:52,616
you put there yourself.

384
00:11:52,796 --> 00:11:54,571
It can feel like you're just moving

385
00:11:54,571 --> 00:11:56,244
money from one pocket to another.

386
00:11:56,594 --> 00:11:58,382
That's why so many people give up

387
00:11:58,382 --> 00:11:58,707
early.

388
00:11:58,887 --> 00:12:00,538
They don't see results quickly,

389
00:12:00,538 --> 00:12:02,243
so they assume it isn't working.

390
00:12:02,423 --> 00:12:04,872
But slow early progress isn't failure.

391
00:12:05,052 --> 00:12:07,258
It's simply how compounding works.

392
00:12:07,438 --> 00:12:09,710
Results stay quiet for a while,

393
00:12:09,710 --> 00:12:10,736
then get loud.

394
00:12:11,085 --> 00:12:12,749
The trick is to measure what you

395
00:12:12,749 --> 00:12:13,373
can control.

396
00:12:13,553 --> 00:12:14,783
Your savings rate.

397
00:12:14,963 --> 00:12:16,217
Your consistency.

398
00:12:16,397 --> 00:12:18,121
The number of months in a row

399
00:12:18,121 --> 00:12:18,893
you invested.

400
00:12:19,073 --> 00:12:20,903
Those numbers move right away,

401
00:12:20,903 --> 00:12:22,916
even when the balance seems slow.

402
00:12:23,266 --> 00:12:24,033
Try this.

403
00:12:24,213 --> 00:12:25,982
Track your months in a row of

404
00:12:25,982 --> 00:12:27,324
saving, like a streak.

405
00:12:27,504 --> 00:12:29,898
Many people find that not wanting to

406
00:12:29,898 --> 00:12:32,425
break a streak is a stronger motivator

407
00:12:32,425 --> 00:12:34,354
than watching a slow balance.

408
00:12:34,704 --> 00:12:36,016
Lesson number nine.

409
00:12:36,196 --> 00:12:38,321
We speed up as goals get close.

410
00:12:38,670 --> 00:12:41,619
Psychologists call it the goal gradient effect.

411
00:12:41,799 --> 00:12:43,357
In a well known study,

412
00:12:43,357 --> 00:12:44,985
researchers Ran Kivetz,

413
00:12:44,985 --> 00:12:47,958
Oleg Urminsky and Yuhuang Zheng found that

414
00:12:47,958 --> 00:12:51,214
coffee shop customers with reward cards bought

415
00:12:51,214 --> 00:12:53,763
coffee more often as they got closer

416
00:12:53,763 --> 00:12:54,895
to a free drink.

417
00:12:55,075 --> 00:12:56,768
The closer the finish line,

418
00:12:56,768 --> 00:12:58,210
the harder people push.

419
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:00,801
The same study found something clever.

420
00:13:00,981 --> 00:13:03,820
Cards that started with two stamps already

421
00:13:03,820 --> 00:13:06,592
filled in got completed faster than cards

422
00:13:06,592 --> 00:13:08,958
with fewer total stamps but no head

423
00:13:08,958 --> 00:13:09,364
start.

424
00:13:09,544 --> 00:13:12,114
Feeling like you've already made progress makes

425
00:13:12,114 --> 00:13:13,700
you more motivated to finish.

426
00:13:14,049 --> 00:13:16,718
That's why ten thousand dollars works so

427
00:13:16,718 --> 00:13:17,718
well as a goal.

428
00:13:17,898 --> 00:13:19,862
It's big enough to matter but close

429
00:13:19,862 --> 00:13:21,265
enough to feel reachable.

430
00:13:21,445 --> 00:13:24,255
One million dollars feels like a fantasy.

431
00:13:24,435 --> 00:13:26,617
Ten thousand feels like a project.

432
00:13:26,967 --> 00:13:27,780
Try this.

433
00:13:27,960 --> 00:13:30,199
Break your ten thousand into ten blocks

434
00:13:30,199 --> 00:13:31,118
of one thousand.

435
00:13:31,298 --> 00:13:32,920
Color in a block each time you

436
00:13:32,920 --> 00:13:33,353
hit one.

437
00:13:33,533 --> 00:13:35,214
You'll start at block one,

438
00:13:35,214 --> 00:13:37,219
not zero, the moment you have a

439
00:13:37,219 --> 00:13:38,189
thousand saved.

440
00:13:38,539 --> 00:13:39,746
Lesson number ten.

441
00:13:39,926 --> 00:13:42,794
Reaching ten thousand creates a temptation.

442
00:13:43,143 --> 00:13:44,525
Here's a strange thing.

443
00:13:44,705 --> 00:13:46,837
Many people work hard to save ten

444
00:13:46,837 --> 00:13:48,582
thousand dollars, and then,

445
00:13:48,582 --> 00:13:50,649
almost as soon as they reach it,

446
00:13:50,649 --> 00:13:52,135
they spend a big chunk.

447
00:13:52,315 --> 00:13:53,258
A newer car,

448
00:13:53,258 --> 00:13:55,299
a vacation, a big upgrade.

449
00:13:55,479 --> 00:13:57,115
Having the money makes it feel like

450
00:13:57,115 --> 00:13:59,171
they can afford things they couldn't before.

451
00:13:59,521 --> 00:14:02,517
Researchers sometimes call this a wealth effect.

452
00:14:02,697 --> 00:14:04,195
When people feel richer,

453
00:14:04,195 --> 00:14:05,692
they tend to spend more.

454
00:14:05,872 --> 00:14:07,338
And ten thousand dollars,

455
00:14:07,338 --> 00:14:08,981
sitting in a single account,

456
00:14:08,981 --> 00:14:10,681
can feel like a lot of money,

457
00:14:10,681 --> 00:14:12,910
even though it's really just the start

458
00:14:12,910 --> 00:14:13,848
of a foundation.

459
00:14:14,199 --> 00:14:16,295
The fix is to decide in advance

460
00:14:16,295 --> 00:14:18,256
what the ten thousand is for,

461
00:14:18,256 --> 00:14:19,609
before you reach it.

462
00:14:19,789 --> 00:14:21,604
If it's your emergency fund,

463
00:14:21,604 --> 00:14:22,900
it's not a car fund.

464
00:14:23,080 --> 00:14:24,804
If it's your investing base,

465
00:14:24,804 --> 00:14:26,343
it's not a vacation fund.

466
00:14:26,523 --> 00:14:29,024
Rules made in advance are much easier

467
00:14:29,024 --> 00:14:29,564
to keep.

468
00:14:29,915 --> 00:14:30,774
Try this.

469
00:14:30,954 --> 00:14:32,684
Give your ten thousand a name.

470
00:14:32,864 --> 00:14:35,440
Freedom fund, foundation fund,

471
00:14:35,440 --> 00:14:37,415
never broke again fund.

472
00:14:37,595 --> 00:14:40,152
It's surprisingly harder to spend money you've

473
00:14:40,152 --> 00:14:40,486
named.

474
00:14:40,836 --> 00:14:41,857
Quick pause.

475
00:14:42,037 --> 00:14:44,087
If this is changing how you think

476
00:14:44,087 --> 00:14:45,950
about your first ten thousand,

477
00:14:45,950 --> 00:14:48,249
please subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

478
00:14:48,429 --> 00:14:50,220
It's free, and it helps us keep

479
00:14:50,220 --> 00:14:50,971
making these.

480
00:14:51,151 --> 00:14:52,835
Now, lesson eleven.

481
00:14:53,184 --> 00:14:54,624
Lesson number eleven.

482
00:14:54,804 --> 00:14:56,882
Use mental accounting on purpose.

483
00:14:57,232 --> 00:15:00,818
Nobel prize winning economist Richard Thaler described

484
00:15:00,818 --> 00:15:03,142
something called mental accounting.

485
00:15:03,322 --> 00:15:06,146
People treat money differently depending on where

486
00:15:06,146 --> 00:15:07,817
it came from or what label it

487
00:15:07,817 --> 00:15:08,047
has.

488
00:15:08,227 --> 00:15:10,712
A tax refund feels like bonus money.

489
00:15:10,892 --> 00:15:13,423
A salary feels like serious money.

490
00:15:13,603 --> 00:15:15,571
Logically, a dollar is a dollar,

491
00:15:15,571 --> 00:15:17,724
but our brains don't work that way.

492
00:15:18,074 --> 00:15:20,544
Usually mental accounting causes mistakes,

493
00:15:20,544 --> 00:15:22,720
like blowing a refund on things you'd

494
00:15:22,720 --> 00:15:24,425
never buy with your paycheck.

495
00:15:24,605 --> 00:15:26,189
But you can flip it into an

496
00:15:26,189 --> 00:15:26,776
advantage.

497
00:15:26,956 --> 00:15:29,739
Separate accounts with clear labels make money

498
00:15:29,739 --> 00:15:31,856
harder to spend and easier to grow.

499
00:15:32,205 --> 00:15:35,071
Many banks let you open several savings

500
00:15:35,071 --> 00:15:36,541
accounts or buckets,

501
00:15:36,541 --> 00:15:38,231
each with its own name.

502
00:15:38,411 --> 00:15:39,972
One for emergencies,

503
00:15:39,972 --> 00:15:41,768
one for a down payment,

504
00:15:41,768 --> 00:15:42,939
one for travel.

505
00:15:43,119 --> 00:15:44,931
When each dollar has a job,

506
00:15:44,931 --> 00:15:47,279
you're much less likely to raid the

507
00:15:47,279 --> 00:15:48,285
important ones.

508
00:15:48,636 --> 00:15:49,611
Try this.

509
00:15:49,791 --> 00:15:51,992
Open a separate savings account for your

510
00:15:51,992 --> 00:15:53,367
ten thousand dollar goal,

511
00:15:53,367 --> 00:15:55,403
ideally at a different bank from your

512
00:15:55,403 --> 00:15:56,339
checking account.

513
00:15:56,519 --> 00:15:58,347
Out of sight really does help keep

514
00:15:58,347 --> 00:15:59,154
it out of mind.

515
00:15:59,504 --> 00:16:00,735
Lesson number twelve.

516
00:16:00,915 --> 00:16:03,330
Losses hurt more than gains feel good.

517
00:16:03,680 --> 00:16:07,551
Psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky showed

518
00:16:07,551 --> 00:16:10,326
that losses tend to feel roughly twice

519
00:16:10,326 --> 00:16:12,956
as painful as equal gains feel good.

520
00:16:13,136 --> 00:16:15,814
Losing one thousand dollars hurts much more

521
00:16:15,814 --> 00:16:18,616
than gaining one thousand dollars feels nice.

522
00:16:18,965 --> 00:16:21,134
This matters when you first invest your

523
00:16:21,134 --> 00:16:21,856
ten thousand.

524
00:16:22,036 --> 00:16:23,553
Markets fall sometimes,

525
00:16:23,553 --> 00:16:25,993
and a ten percent drop means watching

526
00:16:25,993 --> 00:16:28,631
one thousand dollars disappear on paper.

527
00:16:28,811 --> 00:16:29,982
For a new investor,

528
00:16:29,982 --> 00:16:31,399
that can feel terrible,

529
00:16:31,399 --> 00:16:33,372
and some people sell in a panic,

530
00:16:33,372 --> 00:16:34,604
locking in the loss.

531
00:16:34,954 --> 00:16:36,789
Expect it before it happens.

532
00:16:36,969 --> 00:16:39,231
Market drops are a normal part of

533
00:16:39,231 --> 00:16:40,603
long term investing.

534
00:16:40,783 --> 00:16:44,290
Historically, broad markets have recovered from past

535
00:16:44,290 --> 00:16:45,572
declines over time,

536
00:16:45,572 --> 00:16:48,539
though there's no guarantee and recovery can

537
00:16:48,539 --> 00:16:49,281
take years.

538
00:16:49,461 --> 00:16:51,783
Knowing that in advance makes panic less

539
00:16:51,783 --> 00:16:52,190
likely.

540
00:16:52,539 --> 00:16:53,364
Try this.

541
00:16:53,544 --> 00:16:55,443
Write a note to your future self

542
00:16:55,443 --> 00:16:55,680
now.

543
00:16:55,860 --> 00:16:57,616
When my investments drop,

544
00:16:57,616 --> 00:16:58,739
I will not sell.

545
00:16:58,919 --> 00:17:00,777
I invested for the long term.

546
00:17:00,957 --> 00:17:02,736
Read it the next time the market

547
00:17:02,736 --> 00:17:03,070
falls.

548
00:17:03,419 --> 00:17:04,929
Lesson number thirteen.

549
00:17:05,109 --> 00:17:07,048
Ten thousand changes who you are.

550
00:17:07,397 --> 00:17:09,844
Something deeper happens when you reach ten

551
00:17:09,844 --> 00:17:10,811
thousand dollars.

552
00:17:10,991 --> 00:17:13,388
You stop seeing yourself as someone who's

553
00:17:13,388 --> 00:17:14,265
bad with money.

554
00:17:14,445 --> 00:17:16,488
You become someone who saves.

555
00:17:16,668 --> 00:17:18,340
Someone who invests.

556
00:17:18,520 --> 00:17:20,813
That change in identity often matters more

557
00:17:20,813 --> 00:17:21,631
than the money.

558
00:17:21,981 --> 00:17:24,913
Habit writer James Clear argues that lasting

559
00:17:24,913 --> 00:17:27,112
change often comes from identity,

560
00:17:27,112 --> 00:17:28,112
not just goals.

561
00:17:28,292 --> 00:17:30,140
Instead of I want to save money,

562
00:17:30,140 --> 00:17:32,162
the thought becomes I'm the kind of

563
00:17:32,162 --> 00:17:33,144
person who saves.

564
00:17:33,324 --> 00:17:35,345
Every deposit is a small vote for

565
00:17:35,345 --> 00:17:36,448
that new identity.

566
00:17:36,797 --> 00:17:38,968
And once you believe you're a saver,

567
00:17:38,968 --> 00:17:40,234
decisions get easier.

568
00:17:40,414 --> 00:17:42,593
You don't agonize over every purchase,

569
00:17:42,593 --> 00:17:44,829
because the choice already fits who you

570
00:17:44,829 --> 00:17:45,058
are.

571
00:17:45,238 --> 00:17:47,293
That's why the first ten thousand is

572
00:17:47,293 --> 00:17:47,978
so powerful.

573
00:17:48,158 --> 00:17:48,971
It's proof.

574
00:17:49,320 --> 00:17:50,156
Try this.

575
00:17:50,336 --> 00:17:52,429
Finish this sentence and say it out

576
00:17:52,429 --> 00:17:52,728
loud.

577
00:17:52,908 --> 00:17:54,046
